  "critique": {
   "status": "done",
   "result": {
    "critique": "This backtest does not support the strategy. The validation mean is more negative than the training mean and the no-condition baseline is positive in validation, so the low-vol trigger appears to subtract value rather than add it. Costs were not included; once a stricter cost assumption is applied, both training and validation means become significantly negative. The tail risk is severe, with a worst 10-day window far larger than the entire validation gain, and earnings exposure in 16% of validation trades adds understated risk. The validation sample is small (70 trades, 12 blocks) and the shared validation window raises multiple-testing concerns. The high stale-IV drop count in training also suggests the trigger may be selecting illiquid or stale-priced options.",
    "doubts": [
     "Validation mean (-10.18%) is worse than train mean (-4.44%) and more negative than the no-condition baseline validation mean (+2.02%).",
     "Stricter cost assumption turns train and validation means significantly negative (-16.04%, -22.42%), so results depend on untested cost assumptions.",
     "Worst 10-day window of -1,276.54% exceeds the entire validation gain (-712.45%), implying catastrophic tail risk.",
     "Only 70 validation trades and 12 independent blocks; confidence intervals are wide and include zero.",
     "16% of validation trades had earnings in the window; the x1.20 cost multiplier may understate earnings-related gap/assignment risk.",
     "High number of stale-IV drops in training (400) suggests possible selection bias from illiquid option data.",
     "Shared validation window with prior audits and many variants tested raises curve fitting/multiple testing risk.",
     "No transaction costs, bid-ask spreads, margin or assignment/early exercise costs were modeled."
